*.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) lies at column \\(p(i)\\).  \nEquivalently, for each column \\(j\\) there is a unique row  \n\n\\[\nr_j:=p^{-1}(j)\n\\]\n\ncontaining the uncovered square of that column.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Decomposition into two fixed regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<r_j\\}\\qquad\\text{(squares **above** the hole in their column)},\n\\]\n\\[\nB:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>r_j\\}\\qquad\\text{(squares **below** the hole in their column)}.\n\\]\n\nEvery unit square of the board belongs either to \\(A\\) or to \\(B\\) (the uncovered squares themselves are excluded).  \n\n**Claim.** *Any rectangular tile must be contained entirely in \\(A\\) or entirely in \\(B\\).*  \n\n*Proof.* Suppose a tile \\(T\\) contains two squares \\((i_1,j)\\) and \\((i_2,j)\\) from the same column with \\(i_1<r_j<i_2\\).  \nThen the row interval of \\(T\\) contains \\(r_j\\) and the column interval contains \\(j\\); consequently the uncovered square \\((r_j,j)\\) would lie inside \\(T\\), contradicting the condition that tiles may not cover uncovered squares. ∎  \n\nThus the tiling of the whole board is the disjoint union of a tiling of \\(A\\) and a tiling of \\(B\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Shape of the regions  \n\nFor a fixed row index \\(i\\),\n\n\\[\n| \\{j\\mid i<r_j\\} | = n-i,\n\\]\n\nbecause exactly one column has \\(r_j=i\\).  \nHence row \\(i\\) of \\(A\\) contains \\(n-i\\) squares; the rows of \\(A\\) have lengths  \n\n\\[\nn-1,\\;n-2,\\;\\dots ,\\;1,\\;0 .\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, row \\(i\\) of \\(B\\) contains \\(i-1\\) squares, so the rows of \\(B\\) have lengths  \n\n\\[\n0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the *staircase* Young diagram of shape \\((n-1,n-2,\\dots ,1)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound for a staircase diagram  \n\nWe prove the following lemma.\n\n**Lemma 1.** *Any partition of the staircase diagram \\((n-1,n-2,\\dots ,1)\\) into axis‑aligned rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{C}:=\\{(i,i-1)\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe rightmost cell of each non‑empty row of the diagram.  \nEach cell of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) is an *inner corner*: the cell directly to its right, \\((i,i)\\), is missing (it is a hole), while the cell directly below, \\((i+1,i-1)\\), belongs to the diagram (when \\(i<n\\)).  \n\nSuppose a rectangle \\(R\\) of the partition contains two distinct cells \\((i,i-1)\\) and \\((k,k-1)\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nThen the row interval of \\(R\\) contains rows \\(i,\\dots ,k\\) and the column interval contains columns \\(i-1,\\dots ,k-1\\).  \nConsequently \\(R\\) also contains the cell \\((i,i)\\) (row \\(i\\), column \\(i\\)), which is absent from the diagram.  \nThus \\(R\\) cannot contain two different cells of \\(\\mathcal{C}\\).  \n\nSince \\(|\\mathcal{C}|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary. ∎  \n\nApplying Lemma 1 to \\(A\\) and to \\(B\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }A \\ge n-1,\\qquad \n\\#\\text{tiles covering }B \\ge n-1.\n\\]\n\nHence any tiling of the whole board requires at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let \\(r_j\\) be the row of its uncovered square.\n\n* If \\(r_j>1\\), place a vertical tile covering the squares  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le r_j-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\((r_j-1)\\times1\\) rectangle.\n\n* If \\(r_j<n\\), place a vertical tile covering the squares  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\((n-r_j)\\times1\\) rectangle.\n\nThese tiles are disjoint because they occupy distinct columns, and together they cover every square of the board except the uncovered ones.  \n\nThe total number of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\mathbf{1}_{\\{r_j>1\\}}+\\mathbf{1}_{\\{r_j<n\\}}\\bigr)\n=2n-\\bigl(\\#\\{j\\mid r_j=1\\}+\\#\\{j\\mid r_j=n\\}\\big