truction uses vertical tiles only, which indeed each tile covers exactly one cell of C (the left or right neighbor?). Actually vertical tiles cover all squares above or below the hole, which includes many cells of C? Let's see: For each column j, the vertical tile covering rows 1..r_j-1 includes cells left and right of holes? Actually the vertical tile covers squares in column j only, not adjacent cells. The cells of C are left and right adjacent cells, which are in columns p(i)-1 and p(i)+1, not column j. So vertical tiles do not cover any cells of C. Wait, the vertical tiles are placed in each column j, covering squares above or below the hole in that column. The cells of C are in adjacent columns, not in the same column as the hole. So each vertical tile does not cover any cell of C. So the lower bound argument that each tile can cover at most one cell of C is not used to bound the number of tiles needed for the construction; the construction uses 2n-2 tiles, each covering many squares but none of C. The lower bound says you need at least |C| tiles because each tile can cover at most one cell of C. But if a tile can cover zero cells of C, then the lower bound would be weaker: you need at least ceil(|C|/1) = |C| tiles? Actually if each tile can cover at most one cell of C, then to cover all cells of C (which are 2n-2 cells) you need at least 2n-2 tiles, because each tile can cover at most one cell of C. However, if a tile can cover zero cells of C, you could still have fewer tiles than |C|? Wait, the lower bound argument is that each tile can cover at most one cell of C, but we need to cover all cells of C? Actually the argument is that each tile can contain at most one cell of C, but does the tiling need to cover all cells of C? The set C consists of cells adjacent to holes. Are those cells required to be covered by tiles? The problem states that each unit square is covered by at most one tile. It does not require that all squares except the holes must be covered