eq\\varnothing\\), then there exists an integer \\(i\\) with\n\\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le i\\le d\\); the square \\((i,i)\\) would belong to the tile,\ncontradicting the fact that all diagonal squares are uncovered.\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nR\\cap C=\\varnothing .\n\\]\n\nConsequently either  \n\n\\[\nb<c\\qquad\\text{(the rectangle lies entirely **above** the diagonal)},\n\\]\n\nor  \n\n\\[\nd<a\\qquad\\text{(the rectangle lies entirely **below** the diagonal)}.\n\\]\n\nThus every tile is contained wholly in the upper region  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(r,c):r<c\\},\n\\]\n\nor wholly in the lower region  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(r,c):r>c\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe two regions are disjoint, so the total number of tiles equals the number\nused for \\(U\\) plus the number used for \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the upper region  \n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) squares  \n\n\\[\ns_i=(i,i+1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich lie just above the diagonal, i.e. \\(s_i\\in U\\).\n\n*Claim.*  No tile contained in \\(U\\) can contain two distinct squares\n\\(s_i\\) and \\(s_j\\) with \\(i<j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let the tile be \\(R\\times C\\) with \\(R=[a,b]\\), \\(C=[c,d]\\) and \\(b<c\\).\nIf \\((i,i+1)\\in R\\times C\\) then \\(i\\in R\\) and \\(i+1\\in C\\); similarly\n\\(j\\in R\\) and \\(j+1\\in C\\).\nHence \\(a\\le i<j\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le i+1<j+1\\le d\\).\nFrom \\(b<c\\) we obtain \\(j\\le b<c\\le i+1\\), i.e. \\(j<i+1\\), which contradicts\n\\(i<j\\).  \u220e\n\nThus each tile in \\(U\\) can cover **at most one** of the squares \\(s_i\\).\nSince the \\(s_i\\) are all distinct and must be covered (they are not diagonal),\ncovering the whole upper region requires at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the lower region  \n\nApply the same argument to the squares  \n\n\\[\nt_i=(i+1,i),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich lie just below the diagonal.  A tile situated in \\(L\\) can contain at most\none of them, so at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for the lower region.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Global lower bound  \n\nCombining the two parts,\n\n\\[\n\\text{any tiling uses at least }(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\\text{ tiles}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nLeave precisely the diagonal squares uncovered:\n\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i):1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the following tiles.\n\n*Horizontal tiles.*  For each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle covering all squares \\((i,j)\\) with\n\\(j>i\\).\n\n*Vertical tiles.*  For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_j\\) is a \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle covering all squares \\((i,j)\\) with\n\\(i>j\\).\n\n**Disjointness.**  \nEvery square in a horizontal tile satisfies row\u202f\\<\u202fcolumn, while every square in a\nvertical tile satisfies row\u202f\\>\u202fcolumn; therefore no square belongs to both a\nhorizontal and a vertical tile.  Distinct horizontal (resp. vertical) tiles lie\nin different rows (resp. columns), so the whole family \\(\\{H_i\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\}\\)\nis pairwise disjoint.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nTake any off\u2011diagonal square \\((r,c)\\).\n\n- If \\(r<c\\) then \\(c\\ge r+1\\) and the square lies in \\(H_r\\).\n- If \\(r>c\\) then \\(r\\ge c+1\\) and the square lies in \\(V_c\\).\n\nThus every square not on the diagonal is covered exactly once, and each row and\neach column has precisely its diagonal square uncovered.\n\n**Number of tiles.**  \nThere are \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles and \\(n-1\\) vertical tiles, for a total of  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Minimality  \n\nEquation (1) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while (2) shows a\nconcrete tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Hence the bound is tight and\nthe minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
