 included row \\(i\\) we must have  \n\n\\[\nc_s\\le a_i,\n\\]\n\notherwise column \\(c_s\\) would lie to the right of the hole in that row, contradicting \\(R\\subseteq L\\).  \nHence the column set of \\(R\\) is a prefix \\(\\{1,\\dots ,c\\}\\) for some \\(c\\).  \nConsequently \\(R\\) can contain the right‑most cell \\(r_i\\) only when \\(a_i=c\\).  \nBecause the values \\(a_i\\) are all different, **at most one** right‑most cell belongs to any rectangle.\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(a_i>0\\); each of their right‑most cells must belong to some rectangle.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(L\\).\n\nThe same reasoning applied to the **right side**  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ngives a lower bound of another \\(n-1\\) rectangles: the left‑most cell of each non‑empty right segment lies in a distinct column, and any rectangle inside \\(R\\) must be a suffix of columns, so it can contain at most one such left‑most cell.\n\nAdding the two bounds yields\n\n\\[\n\\text{(total number of tiles)}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction attaining \\(2n-2\\)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place\n\n* a horizontal \\(1\\times a_i\\) tile covering the cells \\((i,1),\\dots ,(i,a_i)\\) if \\(a_i>0\\);\n* a horizontal \\(1\\times b_i\\) tile covering the cells \\((i,p(i)+1),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) if \\(b_i>0\\).\n\nThese tiles are disjoint because they lie in distinct rows and, within a row, the two tiles are separated by the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\).  \n\nExactly one row has \\(a_i=0\\) (the row with \\(p(i)=1\\)) and exactly one row has \\(b_i=0\\) (the row with \\(p(i)=n\\)).  \nHence the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{non‑empty left tiles}}+\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{non‑empty right tiles}}\n=2n-2.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (1) this shows that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Achievable intermediate numbers of tiles\n\nThe construction above uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nIf a tile is a rectangle of size at least \\(1\\times2\\) (or \\(2\\times1\\)), we may split it along a grid line into two smaller rectangles; the split increases the total number of tiles by one while still covering exactly the same set of unit squares.  \n\nStarting from the minimal tiling, repeated splits allow us to increase the tile count by any desired amount up to the total number of covered unit squares, namely  \n\n\\[\nn(n-1)=2025\\cdot2024=4\\,098\\,600,\n\\]\n\nwhich is achieved by using a \\(1\\times1\\) tile for each covered cell.  \nTherefore **every integer**  \n\n\\[\nk\\quad\\text{with}\\quad 2n-2\\le k\\le n(n-1)\n\\]\n\nis realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe smallest possible number of rectangular tiles that Matilda can place on a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid, while leaving exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column, is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\nMoreover, any integer between \\(4048\\) and \\(4\\,098\\,600\\) inclusive can be achieved.  "
