t two distinct tiles** unless one of the intervals is empty:\n\n* If \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) then \\(L_i\\) is empty, and only the right side must be covered \u2192 at least one tile.\n* If \\(\\pi(i)=n\\) then \\(R_i\\) is empty, and only the left side must be covered \u2192 at least one tile.\n\nSince \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, at most one row can have \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) and at most one row can have \\(\\pi(i)=n\\).  \nThus at most two rows may need only one tile; all other \\(n-2\\) rows need at least two tiles.  \n\nTherefore any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-2)+1+1 = 2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\u2003\\(\\qquad\\)\u2003(1)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define two (possibly empty) horizontal tiles:\n\n* **Left tile** (present when \\(i\\ge 2\\)): the rectangle covering the contiguous squares  \n  \\[\n  (i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1).\n  \\]\n  Its dimensions are \\(1\\times (i-1)\\).\n\n* **Right tile** (present when \\(i\\le n-1\\)): the rectangle covering the contiguous squares  \n  \\[\n  (i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n).\n  \\]\n  Its dimensions are \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).\n\nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint:\n\n* Within a given row the left and right tiles are separated by the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\), so they do not overlap.\n* Different rows are disjoint because tiles lie in distinct rows.\n\nEvery row \\(i\\) contains exactly one uncovered square \\((i,i)\\) by construction.  \n\nConsider any column \\(j\\).  \nIf \\(j=i\\) then the square \\((i,i)\\) is the unique uncovered square of that column.  \nIf \\(j>i\\) then the square \\((i,j)\\) lies to the right of the hole in row \\(i\\) and is covered by the right tile of row \\(i\\).  \nIf \\(j<i\\) then the square \\((i,j)\\) lies to the left of the hole in row \\(i\\) and is covered by the left tile of row \\(i\\).  \nThus each column also has exactly one uncovered square.\n\nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left tiles (rows }2\\text{ to }n)}+\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right tiles (rows }1\\text{ to }n-1)} = 2n-2 .\n\\]\u2003\\(\\qquad\\)\u2003(2)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nConstruction (2) exhibits a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nHence the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Application to the given grid  \n\nFor the \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid (\\(n=2025\\)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
