"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Encoding the uncovered squares.**  \n   The condition \u201cexactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column\u2019\u2019 forces the uncovered squares to be of the form  \n   \\[\n   U=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n   \\]\n   where \\(n=2025\\) and \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   Set \\(a_i:=\\pi(i)-1\\;(0\\le a_i\\le n-1)\\); thus \\(a_i\\) is the number of squares to the **left** of the hole in row \\(i\\).\n\n2. **Rows that can contain a given column.**  \n   For a fixed column \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le n-1)\\) the squares of the board that are *available* (i.e. may be tiled) in that column are exactly the rows  \n   \\[\n   S_c:=\\{\\,i\\mid a_i\\ge c\\,\\}\\quad\\text{(the column lies left of the hole)} \n   \\]\n   together with the rows  \n   \\[\n   T_c:=\\{\\,i\\mid a_i\\le n-1-c\\,\\}\\quad\\text{(the column lies right of the hole)} .\n   \\]\n   The unique row where the hole itself lies in column \\(c\\) is the row with \\(a_i=c-1\\); that row cannot be used.\n\n3. **A rectangle covering column \\(c\\).**  \n   Any rectangle that contains column \\(c\\) must consist of a **contiguous block of rows** that does **not** contain the forbidden row \\(a_i=c-1\\).  \n   Consequently the minimum number of rectangles needed to cover all available squares in column \\(c\\) equals the number of maximal contiguous blocks of rows after deleting the forbidden row.  \n   This number is precisely the number of *runs* (maximal blocks of equal bits) of the binary sequence  \n   \\[\n   I_i(c)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{a_i\\ge c\\}}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n4. **Summing over all columns.**  \n   Let \\(\\operatorname{runs}(I(c))\\) denote the total number of runs of the sequence \\(I_i(c)\\).  \n   From the observation above,\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}\\operatorname{runs}(I(c)).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Evaluating the sum of runs.**  \n   For a binary sequence of length \\(n\\) the total number of runs equals  \n   \\[\n   1+\\#\\{\\,i\\mid 1\\le i<n,\\;I_i(c)\\neq I_{i+1}(c)\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{runs}(I(c))\n   =1+\\#\\{i\\mid I_i(c)\\neq I_{i+1}(c)\\}.\n   \\]\n   Summing over all columns gives\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{tiles}\n   \\ge (n-1)+\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}\\#\\{i\\mid I_i(c)\\neq I_{i+1}(c)\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **From column\u2011wise to row\u2011wise differences.**  \n   Fix an adjacent pair of rows \\((i,i+1)\\).  \n   The inequality \\(I_i(c)\\neq I_{i+1}(c)\\) holds exactly for those integers \\(c\\) with  \n   \\[\n   \\min\\{a_i,a_{i+1}\\}<c\\le\\max\\{a_i,a_{i+1}\\},\n   \\]\n   i.e. for \\(|a_i-a_{i+1}|\\) different values of \\(c\\).  \n   Therefore\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}\\#\\{i\\mid I_i(c)\\neq I_{i+1}(c)\\}\n   =\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|a_i-a_{i+1}|.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n7. **A universal lower bound.**  \n   Substituting (3) into (2) yields\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\displaystyle\n   \\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|a_i-a_{i+1}|\n   }.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   The numbers \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); consequently each difference \\(|a_i-a_{i+1}|\\) is at least \\(1\\).  Hence\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|a_i-a_{i+1}|\\ge n-1,\n   \\]\n   with equality exactly when the sequence \\((a_1,\\dots ,a_n)\\) is monotone (increasing or decreasing).  From (4) we obtain the universal lower bound\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); then \\(a_i=i-1\\) (the uncovered squares lie on the main diagonal).  \n   *Left of the diagonal.* For each column \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le n-1)\\) place one vertical rectangle covering the squares \\((i,c)\\) with \\(i>c\\).  This rectangle is exactly the set \\(\\{(c+1,c),\\dots ,(n,c)\\}\\); it is a single contiguous block of rows, so one rectangle suffices.  \n   *Right of the diagonal.* For each column \\(c\\;(2\\le c\\le n)\\) place one vertical rectangle covering the squares \\((i,c)\\) with \\(i<c\\).  Again this is a single contiguous block of rows.  \n   The two families are disjoint and together cover every non\u2011diagonal square.  The total number of rectangles used is\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2,\n   \\]\n   which meets the lower bound (5).\n\n9. **Conclusion for the original grid.**  \n   With \\(n=2025\\) we obtain the minimal possible number of tiles\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\nThus the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
