be a 2x2 rectangle covering all four squares, but that would also cover the holes, which is not allowed. So we need separate rectangles for each of the two squares: each is a 1x1 tile. So total tiles = 2. 2n-2 = 2. So matches.\n\nBut is there a way to use fewer? Could we use a rectangle covering (1,2) only, and (2,1) only: that's 2 tiles. So minimal is 2. So lower bound holds.\n\nFor n=3, grid 3x3. Need 3 holes, one per row and column. Let's try identity holes at (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). Then squares left are all off-diagonal squares: 6 squares. We can cover each column left of diagonal with a vertical rectangle: column 1: squares (2,1), (3,1) (two squares) -> one rectangle. Column 2: squares (1,2), (3,2) are not contiguous: (1,2) above hole (2,2), (3,2) below hole. Actually column 2: rows 1 and 3 are available, but row 2 is hole. So they are two separate blocks: (1,2) and (3,2) are not contiguous; they are separated by row 2. So we need two rectangles for column 2: one covering (1,2) and one covering (3,2). Similarly column 3: squares (1,3), (2,3) are above hole (3,3) -> contiguous block rows 1-2 -> one rectangle. So total rectangles: column1:1, column2:2, column3:1 => 4 rectangles. 2n-2 = 4. So matches.\n\nBut maybe we can do better by using a different permutation of holes. Let's try holes at (1,2), (2,3), (3,1). That is a cyclic shift. Let's see if we can cover with fewer rectangles. Let's draw grid:\n\nRows 1-3, columns 1-3.\n\nHoles:\n- (1,2)\n- (2,3)\n- (3,1)\n\nNow we need to cover all other squares.\n\nList squares:\n\nRow1: col1, col3 (col2 is hole)\nRow2: col1, col2 (col3 is hole)\nRow3: col2, col3 (col1 is hole)\n\nNow consider covering by rectangles. We can try to cover row1 col1 and row2 col1 together: they are in column1 rows 1-2 contiguous, and column1 has no hole in rows 1-2? Actually hole in column1 is at row3 (3,1). So rows 1-2 are free. So we can cover (1,1) and (2,1) with one vertical rectangle covering column1 rows1-2. That's one tile.\n\nColumn2: 